1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It's a transportation system unequaled by any other countries in the world.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:15,000
600,000 bridges, 400 tunnels, 2 million miles of roads.

3
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, Paving America on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,960
Arguably, no structures have done more to change the physical and cultural landscape of America

5
00:00:40,961 --> 00:00:45,840
than the concrete and asphalt ribbons that make up its vast road network.

6
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:57,920
America's roads traverse waterways, push through mountains, across barren deserts.

7
00:00:57,921 --> 00:01:04,920
They connect the metropolitan areas to the suburbs, and the suburbs to the farmlands.

8
00:01:04,921 --> 00:01:11,920
And they've helped transform a nation once fragmented by rugged terrain into a financial

9
00:01:11,921 --> 00:01:12,920
superpower.

10
00:01:12,921 --> 00:01:18,920
Every day you're affected by these paved roads because everything you eat, everything

11
00:01:18,921 --> 00:01:22,920
you touch, everything you buy, sell, everything you do, including going to work, is done on

12
00:01:22,921 --> 00:01:24,920
those paved roads.

13
00:01:24,921 --> 00:01:32,480
The explosive growth brought on by America's road infrastructure has forced cities across

14
00:01:32,481 --> 00:01:37,360
the country to expand on what is already the largest engineered transportation system in

15
00:01:37,361 --> 00:01:43,300
the world.

16
00:01:43,301 --> 00:01:54,800
These expansions represent the culmination of over 200 years of road building evolution.

17
00:01:54,801 --> 00:02:00,040
One of the fastest growing cities in America is Denver, Colorado.

18
00:02:00,041 --> 00:02:06,200
Each day, approximately 100,000 drivers make their commute along I-25 from the suburbs,

19
00:02:06,201 --> 00:02:10,560
headed for Denver.

20
00:02:10,561 --> 00:02:18,040
Just outside the city, I-25 splits off to I-225, which straddles the east side of Denver.

21
00:02:18,041 --> 00:02:27,520
A national study in 1999 labeled the I-25, I-225 interchange as the 14th most congested interchange in the country.

22
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:34,600
This is the corridor that connects the two largest employment centers in the state of Colorado and the Denver region,

23
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,380
the Denver Tech Center to the south and the Central Business District to the north.

24
00:02:38,381 --> 00:02:51,100
To alleviate the mounting congestion along Denver's two main arteries, an ambitious $1.7 billion overhaul began in October 2001.

25
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:03,000
It's called the Transportation Expansion Project, or T-Rex for short, and its name is fitting for its size and scope.

26
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:06,980
T-Rex will pave a total of 17 miles of freeway.

27
00:03:06,981 --> 00:03:10,156
The scope of the T-Rex project is huge.

28
00:03:10,157 --> 00:03:16,020
On a daily basis, we're doing a little bit over a million dollars a day.

29
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:25,760
The freeway component will add one additional lane in each direction, and two lanes along the most congested stretches.

30
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:33,460
The majority of the new lanes will be paved in concrete, which will require millions of tons of aggregate.

31
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:40,980
20 miles from the paving site is the quarry where the aggregate is mined,

32
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:43,980
sized,

33
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:48,980
cleaned,

34
00:03:48,981 --> 00:03:51,980
and then transported to a mixing plant.

35
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:03,980
At the mixing plant, aggregate and the other ingredients needed to make concrete

36
00:04:03,981 --> 00:04:07,060
concrete are placed into storage bins.

37
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:14,360
Mixed proportions are controlled by computer and lifted via

38
00:04:14,361 --> 00:04:16,360
conveyor belts to a hopper.

39
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:22,500
Cement and fly ash are added to the mix, which then enters a

40
00:04:22,501 --> 00:04:26,000
large cylindrical drum mixer where it becomes concrete.

41
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:30,200
With tonight's pour, we'll go through about 3,000 tons of

42
00:04:30,201 --> 00:04:33,540
aggregate, which this pile behind me will be gone by tomorrow

43
00:04:33,541 --> 00:04:37,240
Morning.

44
00:04:37,241 --> 00:04:39,940
Approximately 32,000 pounds of concrete

45
00:04:39,941 --> 00:04:41,040
are poured into each truck.

46
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:48,380
It'll take 420 truckloads to finish this night's paving.

47
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:54,000
At the paving site, the freshly mixed concrete

48
00:04:54,001 --> 00:04:56,200
is dumped onto the road bed, where

49
00:04:56,201 --> 00:05:00,040
it waits to be molded into a 24-foot wide, 9-inch thick,

50
00:05:00,041 --> 00:05:01,140
dual lane highway.

51
00:05:03,540 --> 00:05:10,260
This is the task of the slip form paver, a machine that has revolutionized the concrete paving industry.

52
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:20,160
Costing a cool million dollars, a slip form paver is the most expensive piece of equipment at a paving site.

53
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:26,480
But its efficiency and multitasking ability make it well worth the price.

54
00:05:26,481 --> 00:05:32,860
So as the concrete's dumped in front of the paver, it runs through a series of augers

55
00:05:32,861 --> 00:05:38,240
that auger the concrete to either side of the machine.

56
00:05:38,241 --> 00:05:44,000
After that, it goes through a series of vibrators that reconsolidate the concrete into a homogenous

57
00:05:44,001 --> 00:05:47,400
mass.

58
00:05:47,401 --> 00:05:51,660
Sensors located on the paver ensure that the machine is laying down a level and straight

59
00:05:51,661 --> 00:05:53,660
road.

60
00:05:53,661 --> 00:05:55,860
The paver runs off six sensors.

61
00:05:55,861 --> 00:06:01,060
It has four sensors that control the vertical grade of the concrete, two on each side, as

62
00:06:01,061 --> 00:06:07,040
well as two steering sensors that keep the paver in line with where we're trying to pave.

63
00:06:07,041 --> 00:06:12,560
Just behind the augers is the paver's screed, which levels the slab.

64
00:06:12,561 --> 00:06:17,660
But before it's smoothed, the paver pushes steel dowels into the wet concrete every 20

65
00:06:17,661 --> 00:06:19,560
feet.

66
00:06:19,561 --> 00:06:22,920
These dowels provide lateral stability to the roadway.

67
00:06:22,921 --> 00:06:29,920
Finally, the paver uses an automated finisher to smooth the slab.

68
00:06:29,921 --> 00:06:35,920
In 14 days, the dual-lane road will be ready for commuter traffic.

69
00:06:35,921 --> 00:06:45,920
Although this stretch of road is still a work in progress, the completed lanes are already delivering on the promise to ease commuter travel times.

70
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:50,000
We've seen a significant reduction with the improvements that have been made so far,

71
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:54,640
and when we're completed, we should have significant more reduction in congestion.

72
00:06:56,260 --> 00:07:03,500
The economic and social vitality of American cities such as Denver relies heavily on an effective network of roads.

73
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:11,540
This was just as true 200 years ago, when America's first highway, the National Road,

74
00:07:11,541 --> 00:07:16,080
was built to connect the eastern United States to territories out west.

75
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:19,980
Well, the national road was to get us into the territory.

76
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:22,840
And that, of course, is a very American thing.

77
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:26,360
We want to light out for the territory, as Huck Finn said.

78
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:31,240
We've got to resolve our destiny in all that space, our landscape.

79
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:34,000
And so that's what Americans wanted to do.

80
00:07:35,540 --> 00:07:37,720
With financial backing from Congress,

81
00:07:37,721 --> 00:07:42,300
surveyors riding on horseback left Cumberland, Maryland in 1806

82
00:07:42,301 --> 00:07:47,300
and spent the next four years marking a 131-mile path

83
00:07:47,301 --> 00:07:50,200
that would become the National Road.

84
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:58,300
Today, the old National Road is U.S. 40.

85
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:02,460
Right behind me is the old path of the National Road

86
00:08:02,461 --> 00:08:05,260
as it was built in about 1806.

87
00:08:05,261 --> 00:08:09,980
They cut a 20-foot-wide path up and down these mountains a foot deep.

88
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:14,800
And inside of that foot-deep roadbed, they put crushed stones.

89
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:18,080
They would just sit there all day long with a one-pound round hammer

90
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:19,940
and smash stones and throw them into the hole.

91
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:25,800
As soon as sections opened, the National Road was flooded with traffic.

92
00:08:27,460 --> 00:08:31,920
You would have seen Conestoga wagons hauling freight from the west

93
00:08:31,921 --> 00:08:33,700
back to the markets east.

94
00:08:33,701 --> 00:08:40,160
Farmers along the National Road built their homes to raise livestock and crops,

95
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,980
and they eventually turned them into hotels.

96
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,700
And typical of these establishments, they'd have a barn across the street

97
00:08:48,701 --> 00:08:51,480
where they would put the horses and the cattle,

98
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:56,020
and then the men and the women would go across the street, have a nice meal, and go up to bed.

99
00:08:56,021 --> 00:09:03,860
Past Maryland, the National Road moves across western Pennsylvania and into West Virginia.

100
00:09:04,980 --> 00:09:11,500
There, the National Road's finest engineering achievement, the Wheeling Suspension Bridge, crosses the Ohio River.

101
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:18,780
The National Road at that time was so popular it was called the King of Roads, and this was the king's crown.

102
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:21,920
It was the longest suspension bridge ever built in the world.

103
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:25,900
It was 1,010 feet across, and people didn't understand.

104
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:28,200
it could carry 290 tons.

105
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:29,420
What did that mean?

106
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:33,100
That meant 4,000 soldiers marching across the bridge.

107
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:39,180
In 1825, the popularity of the National Road

108
00:09:39,181 --> 00:09:40,840
led the government to expand it

109
00:09:40,841 --> 00:09:43,620
an additional 400 miles west of Wheeling.

110
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:47,800
The second phase of the road was largely surfaced

111
00:09:47,801 --> 00:09:49,240
using the macadam system.

112
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:54,460
The macadam roadway consisted of a shallow roadbed

113
00:09:54,461 --> 00:09:55,420
10 inches deep.

114
00:09:56,020 --> 00:10:00,780
filled with stones no larger than 2 1⁄2 inches wide and 1 inch in diameter.

115
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:07,920
The weight of passing traffic would compact the stones into the ground,

116
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:12,100
making for a hard and relatively water-resistant surface.

117
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:21,000
The National Road is a shining example of what a hard-surface, all-weather road could do.

118
00:10:21,001 --> 00:10:28,420
And as a result, the other states started building these similar type roads into their hinterlands to expand commerce.

119
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:35,600
But road building in America was soon curbed by a new invention, the railroad.

120
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:45,240
The invention of the iron horse would curtail road construction in America for the next half century.

121
00:10:45,241 --> 00:10:55,560
The word turnpike comes from the long wooden pole, or pike, that was used to block passage

122
00:10:55,561 --> 00:10:56,639
across a road.

123
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:02,420
First used for defense, the pike later allowed for the collection of tolls.

124
00:11:04,120 --> 00:11:06,760
Paving America will return on Modern Marvels.

125
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:14,860
Over 90% of America's roads are surfaced with asphalt pavement.

126
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:27,960
Asphalt is a viscous compound that comes from the refining of crude oil.

127
00:11:27,961 --> 00:11:33,480
To make asphalt pavement, asphalt is mixed with coarse and fine aggregate and heated

128
00:11:33,481 --> 00:11:38,520
in a mixing drum.

129
00:11:38,521 --> 00:11:44,600
When cooled it becomes a hard but flexible surface.

130
00:11:44,601 --> 00:11:49,460
With so much asphalt pavement covering America's roads, it's imperative that it can withstand

131
00:11:49,461 --> 00:12:00,640
the millions of cars, trucks and SUVs that grind over it each year.

132
00:12:00,641 --> 00:12:07,680
At the National Center for Asphalt Technology, or NCAT, in Alabama, 14 different state departments

133
00:12:07,681 --> 00:12:14,680
of transportation have their asphalt pavement tortured on a daily basis.

134
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:23,000
But NCAT engineers don't rely on lab tests alone.

135
00:12:23,001 --> 00:12:29,080
They put the pedal to the metal along NCAT's 1.7 mile test track.

136
00:12:29,081 --> 00:12:32,380
What we do is full-scale accelerator performance testing.

137
00:12:32,381 --> 00:12:34,580
We call it full-scale because we don't

138
00:12:34,581 --> 00:12:35,691
use a load simulator.

139
00:12:35,692 --> 00:12:39,340
We use live truck traffic to apply the loads, and it's an accelerated

140
00:12:39,341 --> 00:12:44,560
test because we apply 10 to 15 years of traffic within a two-year period of time.

141
00:12:47,180 --> 00:12:53,040
The NCAT truck fleet is able to accelerate road surface wear and tear by carrying twice

142
00:12:53,041 --> 00:12:57,412
the legal payload permitted for interstate travel.

143
00:12:57,413 --> 00:13:00,560
With five trucks on the track nearly

144
00:13:00,561 --> 00:13:04,964
24 hours a day, five days a week. That's a lot of miles.

145
00:13:04,965 --> 00:13:07,560
And a lot of driving in a circle.

146
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:18,560
I thought I'd go crazy, but it's not that bad. You never get lost. You're never over

147
00:13:18,561 --> 00:13:23,583
a mile and a half from being able to go to the bathroom or take a break.

148
00:13:23,584 --> 00:13:24,560
So all in all,

149
00:13:24,561 --> 00:13:28,480
it's a pretty good step up from over the road.

150
00:13:32,060 --> 00:13:35,320
The test track is broken up into 45 test sections.

151
00:13:36,980 --> 00:13:40,060
37 sections are being tested for material performance.

152
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:43,120
The other eight are tested for thickness design.

153
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:47,360
So if you go out and you build a roadway that's too thin,

154
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:49,420
you're going to have premature failures,

155
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:51,400
and it's going to be very costly to rehabilitate that.

156
00:13:51,401 --> 00:13:53,100
If you build it too thick,

157
00:13:53,101 --> 00:13:55,780
then you've invested money where you really don't need to invest money.

158
00:13:57,460 --> 00:14:01,060
Under the surface of the eight sections are 12 strain gauges,

159
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:02,560
which measure cracking,

160
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:06,000
and two pressure gauges that measure rutting.

161
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:10,560
Once per week, data are collected and analyzed.

162
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:13,800
We call it high-speed data collection

163
00:14:13,801 --> 00:14:17,060
because we're able to sample the gauges at a very high rate.

164
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:19,340
2,000 samples per second is very high.

165
00:14:19,341 --> 00:14:24,820
we can capture very precisely what happens as the pavement is loaded and unloaded.

166
00:14:25,940 --> 00:14:30,240
A big advantage of this kind of research is that we can quantify mathematically

167
00:14:30,241 --> 00:14:33,300
the relationship between pavement response and pavement performance.

168
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:37,840
And once we've quantified that, we have a much more scientific approach

169
00:14:37,841 --> 00:14:40,500
to pavement design and pavement analysis.

170
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:46,300
Today, civil engineers are using tests like this

171
00:14:46,301 --> 00:14:48,720
to discover the secret to the perfect road.

172
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:56,600
But in the late 1800s, hard surface roads were practically non-existent since the railroad dominated travel.

173
00:14:58,180 --> 00:15:03,200
Shortly after the turn of the century, however, America's love affair with the train was starting to wane.

174
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:06,720
People were prisoners to the railroads.

175
00:15:07,060 --> 00:15:09,980
They were prisoners to when they would be able to depart and when they would be able to arrive.

176
00:15:10,440 --> 00:15:15,200
So people were eager to break away from the railroads, but they couldn't because they didn't have the car.

177
00:15:15,201 --> 00:15:20,240
Not until Henry Ford invented the Model T and came out with this mass-produced vehicle

178
00:15:20,241 --> 00:15:22,560
where people were able to truly move about.

179
00:15:23,700 --> 00:15:26,580
The automobile empowered the American consumer.

180
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:32,300
Drivers could go out on their own, when and wherever they wanted,

181
00:15:32,800 --> 00:15:34,620
as long as there was a navigable road.

182
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:39,600
Unfortunately, in the early 1900s, there weren't many.

183
00:15:39,601 --> 00:15:46,600
In 1901, there were only 650 miles of macadam roads.

184
00:15:46,601 --> 00:15:54,600
With citizens demanding paved roads for their motorized vehicles, states started raising

185
00:15:54,601 --> 00:15:57,600
money through taxes to build them.

186
00:15:57,601 --> 00:16:04,600
Road building at the turn of the century was a primitive and laborious process.

187
00:16:04,601 --> 00:16:05,301
process.

188
00:16:05,302 --> 00:16:11,600
Horse-drawn plows and steam shovels were used to clear and grade a right-of-way.

189
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:18,578
Rollers were then brought in to compress the roadbed.

190
00:16:18,579 --> 00:16:20,600
From that point on, there was little

191
00:16:20,601 --> 00:16:25,749
uniformity. The best roads had sub-layers, such as sand or clay.

192
00:16:25,750 --> 00:16:27,600
The final surface might

193
00:16:27,601 --> 00:16:36,000
The final surface might be brick, concrete, or rocks coated with a thin layer of asphalt.

194
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:42,960
By 1910, America had a very incomplete patchwork of paved roads.

195
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:49,520
But a group of automobile enthusiasts wanted to change that

196
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:54,160
by building America's first completely hard-surfaced coast-to-coast highway.

197
00:16:54,161 --> 00:17:05,520
They formed an association that bore the name of their proposed road, the Lincoln Highway.

198
00:17:05,521 --> 00:17:11,580
The proposed Lincoln Highway would start in New York City and pass through 350 cities

199
00:17:11,581 --> 00:17:15,400
and 10 states before ending in San Francisco.

200
00:17:15,401 --> 00:17:19,240
When the route was announced in 1913,

201
00:17:20,120 --> 00:17:22,680
1.3 million cars were being driven

202
00:17:22,681 --> 00:17:25,700
by Americans eager to explore the country.

203
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:29,020
The Lincoln Highway Association said,

204
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:32,060
if you build it, we'll help you.

205
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:33,880
So get out there, build it,

206
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:35,700
and we'll supply you with the cement

207
00:17:35,701 --> 00:17:37,520
so you can make concrete to build the road.

208
00:17:37,560 --> 00:17:40,380
But they were really depending on the towns along the way

209
00:17:40,381 --> 00:17:41,440
to help build the road.

210
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:43,980
But construction on the Lincoln Highway

211
00:17:43,981 --> 00:17:50,720
would cease in 1917 as the country's focus shifted overseas to World War I.

212
00:17:54,520 --> 00:18:00,185
Cement manufacturing stopped. Asphalt manufacturing stopped.

213
00:18:00,186 --> 00:18:03,300
There really was no road building

214
00:18:03,301 --> 00:18:04,317
that went on.

215
00:18:04,318 --> 00:18:10,420
But it also brought trucks onto the highways in unprecedented numbers to carry

216
00:18:10,421 --> 00:18:17,040
goods from the Midwest, places like Ohio, to the port of New York, to take those goods

217
00:18:17,041 --> 00:18:17,741
to Europe.

218
00:18:18,100 --> 00:18:23,340
And what happened was those trucks just tore up the roads because they didn't have pneumatic

219
00:18:23,341 --> 00:18:24,041
tires.

220
00:18:26,260 --> 00:18:31,260
America helped win the war overseas, but at the expense of its roads at home.

221
00:18:32,480 --> 00:18:37,260
Yet the final black eye inflicted upon America's system of roads was yet to come.

222
00:18:37,261 --> 00:18:40,494
To celebrate America's victory in the war, the U.S.

223
00:18:40,495 --> 00:18:47,280
Army organized a highly publicized military convoy to journey across the country along the Lincoln Highway.

224
00:18:48,280 --> 00:18:52,700
A nearly forgotten marker in back of the White House commemorates the historical journey.

225
00:18:53,940 --> 00:18:58,580
This is a zero milestone. From this point, all highways were to be measured.

226
00:18:59,020 --> 00:19:00,454
It was placed here in 1919.

227
00:19:00,455 --> 00:19:03,480
The Secretary of War was here and told them as they left,

228
00:19:03,481 --> 00:19:07,700
go by land across the United States by way of the Lincoln Highway.

229
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:13,500
But it was an arduous trek that took over two months to complete.

230
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:16,940
The Lincoln Highway may have existed on paper,

231
00:19:17,420 --> 00:19:20,820
but in reality, it was an unfinished and dangerous road.

232
00:19:22,500 --> 00:19:27,920
The Lincoln Highway was as much an imaginary line across the nation

233
00:19:27,921 --> 00:19:31,260
as it was concrete and asphalt.

234
00:19:31,261 --> 00:19:33,180
There were seedling miles.

235
00:19:33,480 --> 00:19:35,860
But they only lasted one mile.

236
00:19:35,861 --> 00:19:41,340
As a result, the Army convoy found itself foundering in the mud.

237
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:46,280
Trucks and cars turned over, fell into ravines.

238
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:48,080
Some simply had to be left there.

239
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:54,340
The well-publicized journey brought national attention to the sad state of America's roads.

240
00:19:55,300 --> 00:20:00,980
It would take a collective effort by both the federal government and the local states

241
00:20:00,981 --> 00:20:04,260
to rebuild America's road infrastructure.

242
00:20:08,900 --> 00:20:13,700
In 1903, Dr. H. Nelson Jackson won a $50 bet

243
00:20:13,701 --> 00:20:16,440
when he and co-driver Sewell Crocker

244
00:20:16,441 --> 00:20:19,360
became the first to cross the U.S. in an automobile.

245
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:23,060
The two-month trip from San Francisco to New York City

246
00:20:23,061 --> 00:20:26,500
cost Jackson an estimated $8,000.

247
00:20:28,520 --> 00:20:30,260
Paving America will return.

248
00:20:30,980 --> 00:20:32,080
on Modern Marvels.

249
00:20:39,020 --> 00:20:41,740
Although trucks and cars can put a serious hurt

250
00:20:41,741 --> 00:20:44,900
on a paved road, they have an accomplice

251
00:20:44,901 --> 00:20:46,460
that may be even more hazardous.

252
00:20:48,040 --> 00:20:48,740
Water.

253
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:52,960
Water transformed the dirt roads of yesteryear

254
00:20:52,961 --> 00:20:55,020
into impassable morasses of mud.

255
00:20:56,400 --> 00:20:58,720
Today, it causes potholes and hydroplaning.

256
00:21:00,980 --> 00:21:10,480
But while our ancestors had little choice but to slog through the mud, present day research

257
00:21:10,481 --> 00:21:14,480
is providing civil engineers with preventative know-how.

258
00:21:14,481 --> 00:21:21,160
At the University of Texas at Arlington, civil engineers are studying the characteristics

259
00:21:21,161 --> 00:21:28,220
of rainfall runoff atop an 80-foot long and 20-foot wide concrete road simulator.

260
00:21:28,221 --> 00:21:35,220
The rainfall that you see behind me right now is about 10 inches per hour, and that

261
00:21:35,221 --> 00:21:37,220
is a lot of rainfall.

262
00:21:37,221 --> 00:21:44,960
The test road was built to meet the design characteristics typical of most roads.

263
00:21:44,961 --> 00:21:49,820
This meant placing the concrete road on top of an articulated platform.

264
00:21:49,821 --> 00:21:53,780
Roadways are slanted in two directions, one from side to side to get the water off very

265
00:21:53,781 --> 00:21:59,120
quickly and then in its length to get it down to a collection point.

266
00:22:03,040 --> 00:22:07,860
The primary goal of the research program is to study how different road surfaces can

267
00:22:07,861 --> 00:22:14,260
effectively reduce one of the most dangerous hazards of driving in a rainstorm, hydroplaning.

268
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:19,410
Hydroplaning is a thin layer of water that gets between the wheel and the road.

269
00:22:19,411 --> 00:22:20,111
It cannot

270
00:22:20,112 --> 00:22:22,640
be moved out effectively.

271
00:22:22,641 --> 00:22:25,080
And therefore, when you go to slam on your brakes,

272
00:22:25,081 --> 00:22:26,720
you don't have any friction resistance

273
00:22:26,721 --> 00:22:31,520
between the tire and the road surface.

274
00:22:31,521 --> 00:22:33,820
One of the test surfaces being researched

275
00:22:33,821 --> 00:22:36,260
is a concrete road with horizontal grooving

276
00:22:36,261 --> 00:22:39,440
and a rough-broomed texture.

277
00:22:39,441 --> 00:22:42,200
Putting those grooves on there helps it move down this side

278
00:22:42,201 --> 00:22:44,700
slope to the gutter for collection

279
00:22:44,701 --> 00:22:47,200
and also helps increase the roughness of the road

280
00:22:47,201 --> 00:22:51,680
just slightly to help offset that hydroplaning issue and give you better traction.

281
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:57,840
Upon completion of the research project, the findings will be sent to the Texas Department

282
00:22:57,841 --> 00:22:59,585
of Transportation.

283
00:22:59,586 --> 00:23:06,080
Today, engineers are looking for ways to fine-tune America's roads.

284
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:12,160
But after World War I, American engineers were starting from scratch.

285
00:23:12,161 --> 00:23:19,920
From 1917 to 1919, the heavy trucks needed to transport goods across the country had

286
00:23:19,921 --> 00:23:25,480
destroyed America's road infrastructure.

287
00:23:25,481 --> 00:23:29,400
Rebuilding them started at the federal level, under the jurisdiction of the government's

288
00:23:29,401 --> 00:23:35,160
new chief to the Bureau of Public Roads, Thomas MacDonald.

289
00:23:35,161 --> 00:23:42,100
Thomas MacDonald was, without question, one of the most powerful men in Washington.

290
00:23:42,101 --> 00:23:49,420
And in 1921, MacDonald engineered a highway act made by the federal government,

291
00:23:49,620 --> 00:23:54,560
which put $75 million into the building of highways.

292
00:23:56,080 --> 00:24:01,620
The impact of the act led to over 10,000 miles of roads paved in 1922.

293
00:24:01,621 --> 00:24:08,620
More than three times the construction of the previous five years.

294
00:24:08,621 --> 00:24:14,620
Yet, as soon as it became possible for American motorists to drive on a paved road outside

295
00:24:14,621 --> 00:24:18,620
the boundaries of their cities, another problem arose.

296
00:24:18,621 --> 00:24:23,620
How to get there and back without getting lost.

297
00:24:23,621 --> 00:24:30,620
Few maps contained the hundreds of trail and highway names that adorned the American countryside.

298
00:24:30,621 --> 00:24:33,820
In 1926, the federal government said,

299
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,180
no more names for these roads.

300
00:24:36,181 --> 00:24:38,440
We need a better system, more organized system.

301
00:24:38,680 --> 00:24:40,420
So they decided to number the highways.

302
00:24:40,680 --> 00:24:43,740
But the idea was to organize it so people could follow a number

303
00:24:43,741 --> 00:24:46,800
to any destination they chose or a series of numbers.

304
00:24:47,780 --> 00:24:52,060
And all of a sudden, in 1926, we start seeing black and white shields

305
00:24:52,061 --> 00:24:55,940
popping up all over the country, helping travelers get from point A to B.

306
00:24:55,941 --> 00:25:00,763
The numbering system used even numbers for east and west U.S.

307
00:25:00,764 --> 00:25:02,740
routes, with the smallest

308
00:25:02,741 --> 00:25:08,660
numbers starting in the north and increasing towards the south.

309
00:25:08,661 --> 00:25:13,360
North and south routes were given odd numbers, with the lowest numbers starting in the east

310
00:25:13,361 --> 00:25:17,960
and increasing to the west.

311
00:25:17,961 --> 00:25:23,820
In the 1930s, President Franklin Delano Roosevelt looked to road building to help bring America

312
00:25:23,821 --> 00:25:28,320
out of the Great Depression.

313
00:25:28,321 --> 00:25:32,940
His New Deal programs, such as the Works Progress Administration,

314
00:25:32,941 --> 00:25:38,260
paved 280,000 miles of rural roads by 1938,

315
00:25:38,261 --> 00:25:41,440
while at the same time providing jobs for thousands

316
00:25:41,441 --> 00:25:42,600
of unemployed Americans.

317
00:25:45,340 --> 00:25:53,340
By 1941, America's paved roads reached 600,000 miles.

318
00:25:53,341 --> 00:25:55,440
Yet as road building increased in the United States,

319
00:25:55,640 --> 00:25:59,340
the lack of uniformity in America's roads led to steep grades

320
00:25:59,341 --> 00:26:02,540
and dangerous curves.

321
00:26:06,060 --> 00:26:11,040
Over 30,000 Americans died in automobile accidents in 1940 alone.

322
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:17,360
Thomas MacDonald knew that a major overhaul of the nation's road system was needed.

323
00:26:18,740 --> 00:26:22,760
Thomas MacDonald was invited by the Third Reich to come over and take a look at a new highway

324
00:26:22,761 --> 00:26:23,880
called the Autobahn.

325
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:28,640
And when he came back from Germany,

326
00:26:28,920 --> 00:26:31,460
he and the groups that had gone over to visit this new roadway

327
00:26:31,461 --> 00:26:34,180
were mesmerized by limited access,

328
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:36,640
this idea of separating grades,

329
00:26:36,760 --> 00:26:39,060
keeping dangerous railroad crossings apart,

330
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:41,780
taking local roads and putting it underneath the route.

331
00:26:45,300 --> 00:26:48,800
The first of America's highways modeled after Germany's Autobahn

332
00:26:48,801 --> 00:26:50,780
was the Pennsylvania Turnpike.

333
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:56,760
It was designed as a four-lane divided freeway with limited access,

334
00:26:59,400 --> 00:27:02,320
1,200-foot-long entrance and exit ramps,

335
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:05,960
and no more than a 3% grade.

336
00:27:10,780 --> 00:27:14,120
It took two years to plan and prepare the road for paving.

337
00:27:15,240 --> 00:27:18,900
In 1939, workers laid the first batch of concrete pavement.

338
00:27:18,901 --> 00:27:24,200
Paving continued for one year.

339
00:27:25,880 --> 00:27:31,700
In October of 1940, America's first superhighway opened to the public.

340
00:27:32,860 --> 00:27:36,060
The first day the turnpike opened up, the toll booths were packed.

341
00:27:36,220 --> 00:27:39,600
People were coming from West Virginia to drive on this stretch of road that they had heard about.

342
00:27:39,920 --> 00:27:41,180
And people marveled at it.

343
00:27:41,340 --> 00:27:43,160
It was a party on the Pennsylvania turnpike.

344
00:27:43,161 --> 00:27:51,020
But as the United States became involved in the Second World War, the paving of America

345
00:27:51,021 --> 00:27:57,000
slowed once again.

346
00:27:57,001 --> 00:28:00,940
Yet it was on the front lines in Europe that General Dwight D.

347
00:28:00,941 --> 00:28:02,720
Eisenhower learned firsthand

348
00:28:02,721 --> 00:28:05,600
the strategic importance of an effective road network.

349
00:28:05,601 --> 00:28:11,200
And he ships off and eventually becomes the Supreme Commander of the Allied Forces, battling

350
00:28:11,201 --> 00:28:14,160
Germany along their famed roadway, the Autobahn.

351
00:28:14,800 --> 00:28:19,000
Eisenhower and his armies, once on the Autobahn, were able to pursue the Germans deep into

352
00:28:19,001 --> 00:28:24,400
Germany so quickly that the Germans were sabotaging their own highway behind them to try to slow

353
00:28:24,401 --> 00:28:25,820
down Eisenhower's advance.

354
00:28:27,400 --> 00:28:28,100
Dwight D.

355
00:28:28,101 --> 00:28:33,100
Eisenhower, who had been part of the military convoy in 1919 that slogged its

356
00:28:33,101 --> 00:28:38,500
way across the country along the Lincoln Highway, marveled at the design of Germany's Autobahn.

357
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:46,200
In 1952, Eisenhower was elected president of the United States

358
00:28:46,201 --> 00:28:49,200
and soon began pushing forth a national system

359
00:28:49,201 --> 00:28:52,200
of interstate and defense highways.

360
00:28:52,201 --> 00:28:55,200
Eisenhower was concerned about the loss of life on the highways.

361
00:28:55,201 --> 00:28:58,200
Route 66 was known as Bloody 66.

362
00:28:58,201 --> 00:29:00,200
These two-lane highways were incredibly dangerous,

363
00:29:00,201 --> 00:29:04,200
and he knew that this interstate system with a divided highway

364
00:29:04,201 --> 00:29:08,200
would be the safest route for Americans to travel on.

365
00:29:08,201 --> 00:29:15,200
Eisenhower approved the historic Federal Highway Act of 1956 in June of that year.

366
00:29:15,201 --> 00:29:24,200
The act provided federal funds to cover 90% of states' costs to build their portions of the interstate system.

367
00:29:24,201 --> 00:29:33,200
Known as Ike's Grand Plan, it would soon revolutionize the social and economic landscape of America.

368
00:29:33,201 --> 00:29:40,320
Even the state of Hawaii has interstate highways.

369
00:29:40,321 --> 00:29:45,840
All four are on the island of Oahu and are strategically positioned to connect the multitude

370
00:29:45,841 --> 00:29:49,480
of military bases located there.

371
00:29:49,481 --> 00:30:00,640
Paving America will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

372
00:30:00,641 --> 00:30:05,820
There are over 2 million miles of paved roads in America, and each state is responsible

373
00:30:05,821 --> 00:30:08,020
for maintaining its portion.

374
00:30:08,021 --> 00:30:15,080
For Texas, that adds up to 186,000 miles, a monumental chore.

375
00:30:15,081 --> 00:30:20,760
Thankfully, that job has become a lot easier with the help of this.

376
00:30:20,761 --> 00:30:26,980
The vehicle behind me is a Texas Department of Transportation vehicle, and we call it

377
00:30:26,981 --> 00:30:31,900
the TMV, which is the TxDOT modular vehicle. Other departments of

378
00:30:31,901 --> 00:30:35,860
transportation also have their own vehicles, but none that are as flexible

379
00:30:35,861 --> 00:30:39,700
and collect as many types of data as this one vehicle right here.

380
00:30:39,701 --> 00:30:45,740
The TxDOT modular vehicle uses an ultrasonic sensor to analyze pavement

381
00:30:45,741 --> 00:30:54,540
rutting, a line scan camera to count pavement cracks, and a texture laser to

382
00:30:54,541 --> 00:31:01,540
measure surface friction, all while traveling at highway speeds.

383
00:31:05,040 --> 00:31:10,320
By streamlining road survey technology into one unit, civil engineers can now analyze

384
00:31:10,321 --> 00:31:14,680
stretches of road faster and more effectively than ever before.

385
00:31:14,681 --> 00:31:20,597
This road right here has lots of distress and it's quite rough as well.

386
00:31:20,598 --> 00:31:21,680
The automated

387
00:31:21,681 --> 00:31:24,600
crack system is doing its job and is picking up this distress.

388
00:31:25,120 --> 00:31:29,120
These are the real-time images of what the camera is seeing.

389
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:33,400
Move over to the right a little bit to get some of that lane stripe.

390
00:31:34,860 --> 00:31:36,100
Man, this is a terrible road.

391
00:31:36,660 --> 00:31:39,700
I'm not an engineer, but if this were my opinion,

392
00:31:39,800 --> 00:31:42,300
I would say that they need to seal the cracks or do a seal code.

393
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:48,180
The data are sent to the department's mainframe computer,

394
00:31:48,181 --> 00:31:53,380
where they're analyzed and used to prioritize which state roads are most in need of maintenance.

395
00:31:56,960 --> 00:32:02,820
Although maintaining today's highways requires specialized equipment like the TxDOT modular vehicle,

396
00:32:04,500 --> 00:32:09,440
back in 1956, an entire industry of earth-moving equipment was launched

397
00:32:09,441 --> 00:32:14,960
to move the billions of cubic yards of dirt needed to build America's interstate system.

398
00:32:14,961 --> 00:32:19,960
Eisenhower saw the interstate system as a way to answer the country's most urgent needs.

399
00:32:19,961 --> 00:32:25,960
If there was a nuclear attack, it would be the way that the army would go to ground zero.

400
00:32:25,961 --> 00:32:28,960
It would be a way for people to evacuate the city.

401
00:32:28,961 --> 00:32:30,627
He saw it as a way of commerce.

402
00:32:30,628 --> 00:32:33,960
He knew that this would do a lot for the country commercially.

403
00:32:35,960 --> 00:32:40,960
To establish the acceptable standards and materials for America's new interstate system,

404
00:32:40,961 --> 00:32:46,920
The government spent $27 million on a seven-mile test track in Ottawa, Illinois

405
00:32:46,921 --> 00:32:53,320
to test thickness variances of Portland cement concrete and asphalt concrete pavements.

406
00:32:57,480 --> 00:33:06,060
In November of 1958, soldiers drove along the highway to nowhere around the clock for two straight years.

407
00:33:06,061 --> 00:33:11,200
What it did was it proved what surfaces were the best.

408
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:18,900
So all of a sudden, the federal government is dictating the types of materials that will be used.

409
00:33:19,200 --> 00:33:22,940
They all had to now meet federal specifications.

410
00:33:24,160 --> 00:33:26,820
A new numbering system was devised as well,

411
00:33:27,280 --> 00:33:28,414
to ensure that the U.S.

412
00:33:28,415 --> 00:33:31,420
route numbers wouldn't cross with the new interstate numbers.

413
00:33:31,421 --> 00:33:38,260
The scheme for numbering the new system of highways was to do the same as the one from

414
00:33:38,261 --> 00:33:40,250
the 1920s, but in reverse.

415
00:33:40,251 --> 00:33:45,300
Interstate highways running east and west were given even numbers,

416
00:33:45,301 --> 00:33:49,540
with the highest numbers starting in the north and decreasing to the south.

417
00:33:51,140 --> 00:33:53,800
North and south interstates were assigned odd numbers,

418
00:33:54,020 --> 00:33:57,340
with the lowest numbers in the west and increasing to the east.

419
00:33:57,341 --> 00:34:05,980
In 1956, construction on Eisenhower's Ederstate system began, putting in motion the largest

420
00:34:05,981 --> 00:34:08,820
earth-moving project in the history of the world.

421
00:34:12,860 --> 00:34:16,600
Huge excavators dug a rough path 200 feet wide.

422
00:34:18,360 --> 00:34:20,720
Bulldozers stripped the area of its topsoil.

423
00:34:22,740 --> 00:34:26,940
Scrapers dug out millions of tons of earth, which were used to create the roadbed.

424
00:34:27,340 --> 00:34:34,120
Machines sprayed a thin layer of hot liquid asphalt, which prevented ground and surface

425
00:34:34,121 --> 00:34:36,600
water from creeping into the foundation.

426
00:34:36,601 --> 00:34:43,220
Finally, trucks poured out the roadway's base and final surface, either asphalt or

427
00:34:43,221 --> 00:34:46,380
a layer of concrete poured around rebar.

428
00:34:46,381 --> 00:34:50,140
The uniformity of the interstate system is its success.

429
00:34:50,141 --> 00:34:55,440
The road is no more than a 6% grade out in the mountains, no more than a 5% grade in

430
00:34:55,441 --> 00:35:00,273
city. The interstate system's lanes are 12 feet wide each.

431
00:35:00,274 --> 00:35:02,440
There's a 10-foot shoulder

432
00:35:02,441 --> 00:35:06,058
on your right, a 3-foot shoulder on your left.

433
00:35:06,059 --> 00:35:09,440
The road is straight as it possibly can be.

434
00:35:09,441 --> 00:35:14,440
And all of that means when you're driving down the road, there's no unpredictability.

435
00:35:14,441 --> 00:35:22,440
From 1956 to 1970, concrete pavement was the preferred material used to surface America's

436
00:35:22,441 --> 00:35:26,680
America's interstate system.

437
00:35:26,681 --> 00:35:32,080
But by the 1970s, the concrete that had paved nearly 60% of the interstate was beginning

438
00:35:32,081 --> 00:35:37,140
to show signs of wear and tear.

439
00:35:37,141 --> 00:35:42,760
Asphalt pavement would complete much of the remaining interstate system, as well as resurfacing

440
00:35:42,761 --> 00:35:45,900
portions that were falling apart.

441
00:35:45,901 --> 00:35:51,040
As the infrastructure started aging, you're faced with a decision that you have to make

442
00:35:51,041 --> 00:35:55,700
of removing and replacing concrete, which is a very costly and time-consuming thing to do.

443
00:35:56,480 --> 00:36:03,380
As those decisions started being made, you see asphalt coming more on the scene.

444
00:36:05,800 --> 00:36:11,160
One of the greatest benefits to using asphalt pavement is that it can be driven on as soon as it's laid,

445
00:36:11,580 --> 00:36:13,800
unlike concrete, which takes days to cure.

446
00:36:15,580 --> 00:36:17,680
Asphalt has another key benefit as well.

447
00:36:17,681 --> 00:36:23,400
The wonderful thing about asphalt is it is 100% recyclable.

448
00:36:23,980 --> 00:36:28,740
Each year we pick up about 65 million tons of asphalt.

449
00:36:29,520 --> 00:36:32,520
Of the 65 million tons that we pick up,

450
00:36:32,521 --> 00:36:38,440
about 80% of the 65 million tons is recycled back into asphalt pavement.

451
00:36:40,140 --> 00:36:43,500
Asphalt pavement, like concrete, is produced at a mixing plant.

452
00:36:43,501 --> 00:36:49,480
aggregate comes in from a quarry and is dried inside a large mixing drum

453
00:36:49,481 --> 00:36:54,380
from there the petroleum product liquid asphalt enters the mix

454
00:36:54,381 --> 00:37:00,740
inside the drum the mixture is heated to approximately 320 degrees

455
00:37:00,741 --> 00:37:06,840
the resulting asphalt paving material is then moved by a conveyor belt to a silo

456
00:37:06,841 --> 00:37:12,220
where it's loaded into trucks that bring it to the paving site

457
00:37:13,500 --> 00:37:19,680
At the paving site, trucks pour the mix into a material transfer device that feeds it into

458
00:37:19,681 --> 00:37:22,680
the asphalt paver.

459
00:37:22,681 --> 00:37:28,780
The paver's augers spread the mix out evenly.

460
00:37:28,781 --> 00:37:34,540
A screed shapes the pavement to its desired thickness.

461
00:37:34,541 --> 00:37:37,900
Rollers compress the asphalt mix.

462
00:37:37,901 --> 00:37:42,420
Engineers check to make sure it's level.

463
00:37:42,421 --> 00:37:49,080
place lane lines, and a new stretch of road is ready for traffic.

464
00:37:49,081 --> 00:37:55,180
During the last 50 years of the 20th century, approximately 70% of America's nearly 2.5

465
00:37:55,181 --> 00:38:01,700
million miles of hard surface roads were built.

466
00:38:01,701 --> 00:38:06,700
Rural roads were connected to the cities, which in turn connected to the interstate.

467
00:38:06,701 --> 00:38:13,700
Half a century after the Interstate System Project began, the federal funding appropriated

468
00:38:13,701 --> 00:38:20,640
by Eisenhower's grand plan continues in Boston with the Big Dig, the most complex system

469
00:38:20,641 --> 00:38:23,460
of tunnels ever built.

470
00:38:23,461 --> 00:38:31,640
The longest transcontinental interstate highway in America is I-90, which stretches a total

471
00:38:31,641 --> 00:38:37,540
of 3,087 miles from Seattle, Washington to Boston, Massachusetts.

472
00:38:39,080 --> 00:38:44,880
Paving America will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

473
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:55,600
Although America's interstate system consists of only 2% of the country's total street and

474
00:38:55,601 --> 00:38:59,880
highway mileage, it carries over 20% of the nation's traffic.

475
00:39:01,640 --> 00:39:04,460
a testament to the efficiency and connectivity of the system.

476
00:39:06,200 --> 00:39:11,880
But the only interstate project currently receiving federal funds from Eisenhower's grand plan

477
00:39:11,881 --> 00:39:15,480
is Boston's multi-billion dollar big dig.

478
00:39:16,680 --> 00:39:19,580
It may be the system's finest technological achievement.

479
00:39:20,540 --> 00:39:27,760
The Massachusetts Turnpike Authority built its portion of I-90 actually before there was the Eisenhower plan for the interstate.

480
00:39:27,761 --> 00:39:30,636
but it had one flaw. It didn't complete itself.

481
00:39:30,637 --> 00:39:34,980
It didn't do that last two-mile segment to Logan International Airport.

482
00:39:35,360 --> 00:39:40,400
So the big dig, part of it was to finish that connection, finish that roadway, finish the Eisenhower system.

483
00:39:42,020 --> 00:39:46,820
Besides the tunnels, Boston's big dig also boasts another technological model,

484
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:51,620
Intelligent Transportation Systems, or ITS.

485
00:39:51,621 --> 00:40:00,860
America's roads are entering the digital age, as the hardware that built the interstate system is now being supplanted with software.

486
00:40:01,480 --> 00:40:07,260
ITS is basically there to help us run the roadway smarter, more efficient, more effectively,

487
00:40:07,620 --> 00:40:13,080
to try to reduce the traffic queuing or the backup we often see in major cities.

488
00:40:14,940 --> 00:40:21,040
One of the key ways of preventing freeway congestion is identifying traffic incidents as soon as possible.

489
00:40:21,620 --> 00:40:24,180
and removing them before they cause severe delays.

490
00:40:27,160 --> 00:40:30,700
Our tunnel system has approximately 225 active cameras

491
00:40:30,701 --> 00:40:32,000
at this point in time.

492
00:40:32,001 --> 00:40:34,060
We've divided them up with strategic shots

493
00:40:34,061 --> 00:40:36,640
so we can take a quick glance at the overall roadway

494
00:40:36,641 --> 00:40:39,200
to see if we have a problem.

495
00:40:39,201 --> 00:40:41,880
The operators are assigned to a specific roadway.

496
00:40:41,881 --> 00:40:43,700
Their objective then is to zero in on that

497
00:40:43,701 --> 00:40:45,440
and see if any sort of problem might be coming up

498
00:40:45,441 --> 00:40:47,380
on that section of the roadway.

499
00:40:47,381 --> 00:40:50,280
State police reporting a disabled motor vehicle,

500
00:40:50,281 --> 00:40:51,800
Lever connected northbound, sir.

501
00:40:52,660 --> 00:40:56,920
If the incident is minor, such as a flat tire or non-fatal accident,

502
00:40:57,340 --> 00:41:03,040
an operator will notify an incident response operator, or IRO, to take care of the problem.

503
00:41:03,640 --> 00:41:09,680
1856. Disable motor vehicle, 93 northbound, lever connected.

504
00:41:11,160 --> 00:41:16,900
The incident response operators are placed in strategic locations throughout the metropolitan highway system,

505
00:41:17,060 --> 00:41:18,440
perched, ready to go.

506
00:41:18,441 --> 00:41:21,320
So once we find the problem, we want to get it out in 15 minutes.

507
00:41:21,400 --> 00:41:24,400
And if you don't have them pre-positioned, the chances of doing that are very, very slim.

508
00:41:26,080 --> 00:41:31,700
In addition to the first responders, the control center also utilizes variable message signs

509
00:41:31,701 --> 00:41:38,320
and can broadcast emergency messages directly to commuters traveling through the tunnels.

510
00:41:38,960 --> 00:41:44,180
All the radio, AM and FM radio that go in there, is basically brought in artificially.

511
00:41:44,181 --> 00:41:48,080
You're not getting it from your own antenna. We're basically relaying it down there.

512
00:41:48,440 --> 00:41:54,540
During an emergency, I can have that shut off and then pump into the tunnel our own transmission,

513
00:41:54,700 --> 00:41:57,680
saying that we have such and such emergency and give you instructions.

514
00:41:59,580 --> 00:42:03,760
The Big Diggs Operations Control Center is still a work in progress.

515
00:42:04,600 --> 00:42:05,840
But one thing appears certain.

516
00:42:07,860 --> 00:42:10,780
Intelligent transportation systems are here to stay.

517
00:42:11,100 --> 00:42:16,380
An operations center like this is basically starting to use the roadway as smartly as we can, more effectively.

518
00:42:16,381 --> 00:42:23,380
That will be a never-ending quest, and technology will help us bring it to bear.

519
00:42:23,381 --> 00:42:31,880
The completion of Boston's Big Dig marks a total of 42,795 miles of freeway, funded by

520
00:42:31,881 --> 00:42:36,380
Eisenhower's Grand Plan of Interstate and Defense Highways.

521
00:42:36,381 --> 00:42:44,660
But the expansion of America's interstate system is not solely reliant on federal funds.

522
00:42:44,661 --> 00:42:48,540
taxes and tolls are paying for the next generation of interstate construction.

523
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:57,980
Although Ike's grand plan is still ongoing, its impact was felt soon after it began.

524
00:42:58,980 --> 00:43:03,780
By the 1970s, more people lived in the suburbs than in the cities.

525
00:43:05,060 --> 00:43:09,620
And just as the National Road gave birth to prosperous enterprises in the late 19th century,

526
00:43:09,621 --> 00:43:16,620
The superhighways of the 20th century spurred the development of massive shopping centers,

527
00:43:16,621 --> 00:43:20,620
office parks, hotels, and chain restaurants.

528
00:43:20,621 --> 00:43:28,620
What the road system did was it's made it possible for us to get out into the landscape

529
00:43:28,621 --> 00:43:31,620
and given us more mobility.

530
00:43:31,621 --> 00:43:36,620
America's road system has really changed our economy and the way we live.

531
00:43:36,621 --> 00:43:39,100
It's changed our interior lives.

532
00:43:39,620 --> 00:43:44,560
We now think in terms of time and space, in terms of minutes.

533
00:43:44,920 --> 00:43:47,840
We don't think of it in terms of the distance.

534
00:43:49,040 --> 00:43:52,440
America's roads conquered vast distances and rugged terrain,

535
00:43:53,300 --> 00:43:57,240
uniting a nation like no other transportation network in history.

536
00:43:57,880 --> 00:44:02,720
But the system itself may be its greatest legacy for generations to come.

537
00:44:02,721 --> 00:44:06,940
The same way we look back at the Romans and the creation of their aqueducts,

538
00:44:06,941 --> 00:44:11,600
people a thousand years from now will look back at our society and see this interstate system

539
00:44:11,601 --> 00:44:16,040
that connected this country more than any single piece of infrastructure.

540
00:44:16,520 --> 00:44:18,660
We will be known for our highways.

541
00:44:32,720 --> 00:44:34,040
Thank you.

